A||||27||Jaei Sun||Next car I get is going to have a Honda engine...||||13||
Z||000000||Jaei Sun||05-05-2001||08:19 PM||||What's wrong with american auto makers these days?  <P>I was just reading of the new Acura RSX (replaces the Intregra) and it's new engine power plant This new Honda engine comes in two flavors:<P>first is the basic version, a 2.0 L DOHC 4 -cylinder with VTEC<BR>- the standard one has intake VTEC and makes 160 HP & 141 ft.-lb.<P>- the performance one(!) has intake VTEC AND exhaust VTEC and makes 200 HP & 142 ft.-lb.<P>200 HP out of a 2.0 L FOUR CYLINDER N/A, WOW I'm VERY IMPRESSED!!<P>These are available with a 5 speed auto with SportShift (autostick) or a 6 speed manual.<P>Imagine when you bolt on a turbo... <P>What engine can any auto maker compare to this?||||reg||13
Z||000001||Black88GT||05-05-2001||08:33 PM||||The new iVtec system is awesome.  Its for the intake valves and exhaust valves (previously just intake valves).  I am seriously considering dropping one of these motors into a CRX, thats the first thing I thought of when I read about the new engine.||||reg||14
Z||000002||88formula||05-05-2001||08:37 PM||||The problem is that those high tech engines cost considerably more than most American designs. The big three are cheap and only care about making money. They are just bean counters that will make a part as cheap as they can to increase their profit margins. You know what happened to the fiero! This is why foreign manufacturers are kicking our buts in reliability and quality. Most of these engines and drive trains are superior to American designs. Ask any machinist who has rebuilt both foreign and American engines and he will tell you the same thing.<P>Now the foreign manufacturers are threatening the big three’s bread and butter (truck market) with the Toyota Tundra. I think that truck’s DOHC 32 valve power train is also superior to Chevy, Ford, and Chrysler designs. I should know I have a Tundra and I also have a lot of time behind the wheel of American trucks. American trucks can haul a lot of weight but for quality, reliability, and performance they just suck.     <P><BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||p4n1c||05-05-2001||08:53 PM||||Why don't you just get a Oldsmobile Quad 4?  2.3l 180hp for the H/O version of the engine with 160 ft/lbs of torque.  Normal version of the engine is 160hp.  <P>Don't put your faith in Crapda yet.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>1988 Olds Cutlass Supreme International||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnnyK||05-05-2001||09:07 PM||||A quad 4.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> hehe.. It's just not the same as a nice, smooth as hell japanese engine. I ESPECIALLY love the 3.0 Nissan engine.. sigh... Love it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fformula88||05-05-2001||09:10 PM||||The US automakers tend to use V6's in any car they want decent power in.  They go for the torque output in the bigger displacement engines.  Don't sell the big three too short yet however.  Besides the aforementioned  quad 4, Ford is about to put out a 170HP Zetec four in the Focus.  <P>The downside to the small 4 cylinder engines is they have to be spun at high RPM's to get the power out of them.  If you like to do a lot of spirited driving, I'd rather have a torquey engine that won't be buzzing at 8000 RPM in my ear.  Thats just my personal taste.  Also, I'd rather have an engine thats fast out of the box than one you have to buy all kinds of aftermarket stuff for to make fast.  Again, my taste as I know a lot of people live to mod out their engines.<P>Just keep in mind, there is no replacement for displacement!  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Pontiaddict||05-05-2001||09:13 PM||||What about 350 hp from a pontiac superduty 4?<BR>16 years ago...<BR>No fancy valve timing crap either.<BR>And it bolts right into a fiero.<BR>And the engine is capable of way more hp before it even thinks it's actually working.<BR>(500hp N/A, I've heard)<BR>Sure it cost's more, but it's basically a race motor, you get what you pay for.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Black88GT||05-05-2001||09:20 PM||||Yeah you have to wind up the smaller motors but thats just their nature.  My Fiero's exhaust (Borla no cat.) is considerably louder then my friends stock Civic Si at any RPMs, even when its up around 7,500 (the Civic of course).  Free flowing performance exhausts are loud.<P>There never going to make that fast of a car out of the box.  That will elminate the import aftermarket which is bad for the economy.  It's business.<P>Am I the only one that rolls their eyes everytime they hear there's no replacement for displacement? <P>The SD4 was notoriously unreliable.  Read the Pontiac book by Gary Witzenburg it will tell you everything you need to know.  Also for a complete SD4 your talking over 10,000 bucks (so I have heard from my 2m4 friends)<BR>which you could have a Northstar or a 3800 or a number of other better choices. <p>[This message has been edited by Black88GT (edited 05-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Sleeper||05-05-2001||10:53 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Black88GT:<BR><B><P>Am I the only one that rolls their eyes everytime they hear there's no replacement for displacement? <P>[This message has been edited by Black88GT (edited 05-05-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>nope, i'm so sick of hearing the statement. its so old. ppl don't realize that just because they have a v8 doesn't make them faster. <BR><P>------------------<BR>85 GT<BR>17" Racing Hart C5 wheels w/ Kumho Ecsta Supra 712 tires, Intrax lowering spring, KYB shocks, KYB-GR2 struts, West Coast Fiero Strut Tower Bar, West Coast Fiero Cold-Air Intake w/ HKS Super Flow filter, J.E.T. super chip and MSD Blaster Coil.||||reg||1
Z||000009||frontal lobe||05-05-2001||11:16 PM||||Gotta love those Honda 4-bangers.  Very smooth, too, so not so horribly punishing to wring them out and get some power out of them.  I heard Quad 4's had good output but really punished you with vibration for getting the power out.<P>I have to agree, though, for U.S. driving torque is WAY more useful because of the speed limits.  By the time you get the engine in the power band in second gear, you are on your way to a speeding ticket.  I had that problem all the years I had my Mazda 626 turbo engine.  Great engine, but didn't really get to get to benefit out of it often.  (Plus, torque steer could make for a wild-but fun-ride!)<P>It's been my experience that GM and other domestic automakers could put a v6 in a car for about the same cost as a Honda 4 so for U.S. driving that was more appealing.  Nice to have so many great choices.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Shaft||05-06-2001||12:43 AM||||I was just reading the latest Motor Trend and they had a feature on the new Trail Blazer (vs. the new Explorer). Now we've all probably heard that the Trail Blazers i-6 engine pumps out 270 hp. But many probabaly haven't heard that the engine is probably the quietest and smoothest engine GM has ever built. Plus it's the first SUV to meet the new low-emission standards. How does this relate to this post about 4 cylinders??? Well GM aready has plans for i-5 and i-4 versions of the engine. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||KRMFiero||05-06-2001||09:55 AM||||there is a guy that always has this for sale<BR>"Honda V-Tec Engine Conversion kit forsale; everything you need to convert your none V-Tec Honda to a V-Tec.  $1000.00"<P>thats alot of money to put into a fiero if you dont know it will work,  would be fun though  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||p4n1c||05-06-2001||11:03 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Shaft:<BR><B>Now we've all probably heard that the Trail Blazers i-6 engine pumps out 270 hp. But many probabaly haven't heard that the engine is probably the quietest and smoothest engine GM has ever built.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>This must be the same engine that was already put into the Bravada, I've seen a couple around here but they've all been parked.  It would be fun to take one of those for a spin.<P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>1988 Olds Cutlass Supreme International||||reg||1
Z||000013||Oreif||05-06-2001||12:29 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Black88GT:<BR><B><BR>The SD4 was notoriously unreliable.  Read the Pontiac book by Gary Witzenburg it will tell you everything you need to know.  Also for a complete SD4 your talking over 10,000 bucks (so I have heard from my 2m4 friends)<BR>which you could have a Northstar or a 3800 or a number of other better choices. <P>[This message has been edited by Black88GT (edited 05-05-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I looked thru Gary's book and could find no reference to the SD4 being "unreliable" The SD4 was a pure race engine. Reliable up to 550 horses using top fuel. A 232HP SD4 normally went for $8500 in street form. The engines that went for over $10,000 were the race engines. Actually as for an "under 3.0L" race engine, it was far less cost than the Toyota, Honda, and Mazda racing engines for the IMSA GTU cars. The SD4 IMSA racing Fiero won more races than any other car during it's race career. (They could not race Fiero's once the production cars were stopped) In fact the SD4 and it's clone from KRP are still in use today in ARCA truck racing and many speed boats. Still producing 300+ horsepower reliably.<P>As for import engines. Yes they produce lots of power, yes they have tighter tolerence's, But the tighter it's wound the longevity of the engine decreases. <BR>Beside's those mentioning about "american's can't build a quality car" seem to forget that most import cars are built here by americans!<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Spektyr||05-06-2001||05:20 PM||||Call me shallow, call me fickle, call me whatever.<P>I like motors that growl.<P>I like motors that rumble when they idle.<P>I like motors that can flex the body when you rev them in neutral.<P>I like motors that give the impression your car is some sort of medieval monster ready to breathe fire and injest the car driving slowly in front of you.<P>All of these things are pretty hard if not impossible to acheive in a small motor like a 4 banger.  You need a 6 or 8 to acheive those 'earthquake in motion' kind of exhaust notes.<P>My V6 running without the cat has a nice bass idle that can be mistaken for a V8.  It gets the faintest hint of rice at high rpm, but the throaty growl is still there.  I like it because it sound mean... <I>hungry</I>.<P>But then that's just my opinion...||||reg||1
Z||000015||Black88GT||05-06-2001||06:54 PM||||All quoted from Gary's book ...<P>"What we learned in a hurry was that the harmonics of a 4-cylinder road-racing engine, where you're running up and down thorugh the worst vibration range several times a lap, lap after lap, just destroyed parts left and right.  I think we made it 2 1/2 hours into that 24-hour race before we started pitching cam gears through the cam cover"  P. 96<P>"The Fiero's problems that first year all had to do with reliability. Callies went through six redesigns of cam gears. 'We finally got the cam gear fixed, then we started breaking valves until I figured out how to put a rev kit in it,' says Callies.  'About that time, I finally got our aluminum head finished, and that gave us a power increase.  Then we started breaking pistons, after which we were breaking connecting rods; then flywheels began falling off.  It was just a nightmare"  P.96<P>"While the IMSA Fiero did well, it failed to finish its first four races... 'A rocker arm broke. That was another problem.' " P. 97<P>So, if thats not unreliable well then I don't know what is.  When the designer himself is saying "I was taking the red-eye home every Sunday with a bag of broken parts over my shoulder" then you know something wasn't right.<P>It does say that it won many races and it was good competition.  However I don't see anything about 500+ Hp, the most I see is 370 bhp in a boat and 330 bhp in a car.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Oreif||05-06-2001||10:03 PM||||Per the "Super Duty Build Book" the first designed SD heads had some problems. (weak studs and valve guide problems) these were corrected in the later casts and in the aluminum heads. <P>As for 550hp, it was the Fiero drag race car that had an SD4 in the front and ran on alchohol. Built by "The Rod Shop" using an 85 GT body.<BR>The record for a 4-cyl F/Econo Dragster is Doug Kjono's 475HP SD4 powered Dragster. 1/4 mile 9.0 seconds@140mph.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Philphine||05-07-2001||10:56 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jaei Sun:<BR><B>  <P>I was just reading of the new Acura RSX (replaces the Intregra) and it's new engine power plant This new Honda engine comes in two flavors:<P>first is the basic version, a 2.0 L DOHC 4 -cylinder with VTEC<BR>- the standard one has intake VTEC and makes 160 HP & 141 ft.-lb.<P>- the performance one(!) has intake VTEC AND exhaust VTEC and makes 200 HP & 142 ft.-lb.<P>200 HP out of a 2.0 L FOUR CYLINDER N/A, WOW I'm VERY IMPRESSED!!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>not impressed. i want higher tourque numbers with those hp figures. ||||reg||1
Z||000018||Jaei Sun||05-07-2001||12:05 PM||||You just bolt on a Jackson Racing Supercharger and there's the torqe.  But this is a 2.0 L four cylinder.  A quad 4 is what 2.4 L.  When honda gets up to 2.4 L their engine will be vastly superior to the quad 4.  And a hellva more reliable too (*cough* quad 4 head gasket failure *cough*)||||reg||1
Z||000019||p4n1c||05-07-2001||01:02 PM||||Well you could always get the 2001 Alero OSV engine which is a 4 cyl, 2.4l with a super charger and has 240hp and 240 ft/lbs of torque.  These are at lower RPMs then the Honda engines.  Somewhere around 5500rpm for the hp.  <P>The 2002 looks like it has a 245hp@5600rpm 3.5l V6 though with 255 ft/lb of torque at 4400rpm.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.alero.org/sneakpeek/cafront.jpg">   <IMG SRC="http://www.alero.org/sneakpeek/caback.jpg">  <P>And GM usually gives more torque from their engines, hence the low horsepower numbers.  They usually have high torque, low horsepower.  A balance which I think is good, first you blow them away off the line then hold it as you hit the higher powerband.<P>Just my .02<P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>1988 Olds Cutlass Supreme International<p>[This message has been edited by p4n1c (edited 05-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||loafer87gt||05-07-2001||01:27 PM||||I was just driving my Dad's Camry 3.0 V6 yesterday and what a sweet engine. It is the smoothest engine I have ever had experience with, and has a truly sinister sound to it (the engine, not the exhuast). Not only that, the car has 378,000 km on it and Dad has only done the tire, struts,  and brake pads, and the car still is runny VERY strong. Has anyone driven a 3.5 powered GM car? How smooth is the engine, and how reliable are they supposed to be? I have always thought that it would fun to swap one into a Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000021||p4n1c||05-07-2001||01:55 PM||||Actually, my parents drove the Honda Odessey and said it was the noisest vehicle they have ever driven, they said it had everything they wanted, but it was way to noisey, after talking to some people they got the general concensus that most of the imports are lacking the amount of sound insulation used in the domestic cars.<P>I drove in my friends Camry or Corolla, can't remember which one and found it to be noisey as well.  I have heard Intrigue and Alero's go by, and they are very quiet cruising, under WOT though, they have the sound that means business.  My grandfather has a '99 or '00 Riviera and it is very quiet, but when he gives it gas, you can hear the supercharger and engine pumping.<P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>1988 Olds Cutlass Supreme International<p>[This message has been edited by p4n1c (edited 05-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000022||Black88GT||05-07-2001||03:41 PM||||One of my bosses has an Odessey and I when you turn the key I can't even tell its running.  My brother has a Camry and I think it has like 2 different noise reducers before the muffler (one from the exhaust manifold downpipe, one after the cat I think) and that thing is real quiet.  The only time it wasn't quiet was when the timing belt was going bad.  In general almost any new car is fairly quiet.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000023||p4n1c||05-07-2001||03:53 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Black88GT:<BR><B>One of my bosses has an Odessey and I when you turn the key I can't even tell its running.  My brother has a Camry and I think it has like 2 different noise reducers before the muffler (one from the exhaust manifold downpipe, one after the cat I think) and that thing is real quiet.  The only time it wasn't quiet was when the timing belt was going bad.  In general almost any new car is fairly quiet.  <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sitting idling and driving are two different things.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Sure it's quiet while sitting still, but when they were driving they could only hear road noise, that's the reason they chose the A$$-tek, the Odessey was to noisey and the Montana had drivetrain problems(which is odd considering the A$$-tek and Montana share the same drivetrain).  Our Cutlass was quieter than the Odessey while driving.<P>I'm just telling you what they found from test driving mini-vans and other vehicles.<BR><P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>1988 Olds Cutlass Supreme International||||reg||1
Z||000024||Jaei Sun||05-14-2001||11:37 PM||||Read this on Autoextremist.com recently.  The one good v6 DOHC engine GM makes is going bye-bye.<P><BR>"GM. One of the few bright spots in GM's dismal recent history has been the 3.5-liter double overhead cam V6 found in various Oldsmobiles. This little jewel of an engine will now be phased out when the Intrigue fades away in 2003. They're blaming it on various things, but it comes down to not wanting to spend any more money on it for emissions upgrades, which will be necessary. If GM actually had real live car people running the company, this would never happen. That engine could be and should be used in any number of applications. As a matter of fact, it would make a terrific base engine for the next generation "F" cars. Oh yeah, we forgot...what "F" cars?" <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://home.mn.rr.com/fierojaei/trans_fiero_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000025||Raydar||05-16-2001||12:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jaei Sun:<BR><B>Read this on Autoextremist.com recently.  The one good v6 DOHC engine GM makes is going bye-bye.<P><BR>"GM. One of the few bright spots in GM's dismal recent history has been the 3.5-liter double overhead cam V6 found in various Oldsmobiles. This little jewel of an engine will now be phased out when the Intrigue fades away in 2003. They're blaming it on various things, but it comes down to not wanting to spend any more money on it for emissions upgrades, which will be necessary. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I had heard that this engine was going to eventually replace the 3800.<BR>The reason? Emissions regulations. Go figure.<P><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||1
Z||000026||Fformula88||05-16-2001||04:54 PM||||I would not expect GM to kill the 3.5L DOHC.  They just introduced it, and I would imagine they will find a home for it in at least one of the other divisions.  I've never had the chance to drive one, but everything I have read indicates they are nice engines.<P>I cannot stand Honda's!  I know they get lots of HP out of very little engine, but their cars just don't do it for me.  They are also everywhere.  Every high school and college punk has one these days.  Sure you can make them fast and handle well, but its still a Civic.  Their styling is econo-box boring, and the interiors look and feel like Mom's family hauler.<P>Toyota's new Celica's are nice, and pack some good power.  My friends father has a GT-S with the 180 HP engine.  Its a lot of fun when the cams change over and you get that boost in the upper RPM range.  Its downside is a lot of that power is hard to get to in daily driving.  How often can you go down city streets screamin at 7K RPM?  Not many before the neighbors call the cops!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  ||||reg||1
Z||000027||saleenfiero||05-16-2001||04:56 PM||||VTEC, schmeTEC, I like to have my engine power kick in today, not in 3rd gear or 8000 RPM's.  2 valves, pushrods, and a single cam ALL THE WAY<P>------------------<BR>'86 SE - My car is actually a MacLaren F1 with a Fiero body kit; or not.||||reg||5
